    "cover": "slide-061.webp",
    "category": "Buyer Resources",
    "intro": "Compare screen printing, pad printing, transfer and other decoration methods for different plastic bottle shapes. These checkpoints focus on real sampling, documentation and commercial decisions for the exact plastic drinkware model.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Select the decoration method after the material",
        "paragraph": "A method that works well on a stainless tumbler may be unsuitable for PP or a particular transparent copolyester. Begin with the body resin, surface texture, bottle curvature and how users will hold or clean the product. Ask suppliers for examples produced on comparable plastics. Testing actual ink adhesion matters more than a brochure listing every print process."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Screen printing for straightforward graphics",
        "paragraph": "Screen printing can work for clear single- or limited-color artwork on appropriate bottle surfaces, subject to surface treatment, ink chemistry and a sufficiently consistent print area. It may be cost-effective at volume, but artwork setup and minimums can increase with colors. Curvature and protruding features can constrain placement. Request a printable-size drawing and sample before offering unlimited wraparound designs."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Pad printing for smaller or irregular areas",
        "paragraph": "A flexible pad can transfer artwork onto certain curved parts or lids, but feasibility depends on geometry, substrate and ink system. Small multi-color marks may require careful registration. Buyers should ask for repeatability and wear checks on the real component, not assume pad printing performs identically on a body, cap and handle."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Transfers and other full-color techniques",
        "paragraph": "Heat transfers, UV-based methods and other digital decoration systems may suit more complex graphics, but some processes or curing conditions can affect plastic surfaces. A premium photographic effect in a design mockup is not evidence it can be produced on every resin. Confirm layer adhesion, durability, color reproduction and sample availability before selecting a method for bulk orders."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Define the approval standards",
        "paragraph": "Supply editable artwork, color references, desired logo dimensions, print positions and the bottle orientation that customers will see. Evaluate printed samples for sharp edges, correct registration, scratches and rub performance under intended handling. Keep an approved golden sample. If two bottle sizes need different print placements, make separate production files and approval records."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Understand pricing and rights",
        "paragraph": "Ask how many colors and locations are included, the setup cost, per-unit decoration cost, spoilage allowance and minimum quantity per design. State who owns the artwork and whether subcontractors may use it for other jobs. When reordering, confirm the same production process and material surface to avoid changes in logo appearance."
      }
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Share the actual resin and surface finish",
      "Provide editable vector logo files",
      "Confirm printable area and method compatibility",
      "Approve durability and color of a real printed sample",
      "Separate setup costs from unit decoration fees"
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Is laser engraving appropriate for plastic water bottles?",
        "answer": "It depends strongly on resin and process and is not a universal alternative to printing. Ask the supplier to verify compatibility and any safety or surface changes for the exact product."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can a full-color logo be printed on a curved bottle?",
        "answer": "Possibly, but usable methods and image quality depend on curvature, texture, plastic grade and quantity. Evaluate a decorated sample rather than only a rendering."
      }
    ],
