  {
    "slug": "plastic-water-bottle-food-contact-compliance-checklist",
    "title": "Plastic Water Bottle Compliance: Food-Contact Documents Buyers Should Request",
    "description": "Importing reusable plastic bottles? Check material declarations, food-contact test scope, migration conditions and traceability before approving bulk orders.",
    "primary_keyword": "plastic water bottle compliance",
    "secondary_keywords": [
      "food contact compliance plastic bottles",
      "FDA food contact materials",
      "EU plastic food contact declaration",
      "water bottle compliance documents"
    ],
    "cover": "/assets/blog/plastic-water-bottle-food-contact-compliance-20261018.webp",
    "coverAlt": "Clear reusable plastic sports bottle with a separate blue lid and gasket on a quality-control lab workbench; illustrative buyer-guide image",
    "category": "Quality & Safety",
    "intro": "A supplier saying a bottle is food safe is a starting point, not a complete purchasing record. Importers need evidence that matches the actual bottle, its components, intended use and destination market. A clear body, colored cap, straw and seal may use different materials, and a report for one resin or sample does not automatically cover every finished configuration. This guide turns a broad compliance request into a practical document checklist. It is procurement guidance, not a certification of any XUYI product; the buyer should confirm applicable legal requirements for the exact market and use.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Start with the destination market and intended use",
        "paragraph": "Before asking for a certificate, define where the bottle will be sold and how it will be used. Specify whether it is intended for cold or room-temperature drinks, repeated use, a straw or spout, and any cleaning or temperature conditions that matter. The United States and European Union do not use one interchangeable approval label for every plastic bottle. Requirements depend on the applicable rules, the substances in the material, the finished article and the conditions of contact. A supplier can only respond precisely when the market and use case are clear."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Map every component that can contact a drink",
        "paragraph": "Request a component list for the bottle body, cap, straw, gasket, valve and any other part that may touch the liquid during normal use. Ask separately about colorants, additives, coatings or printing where relevant, including whether decoration is on an exterior or contact surface. A declaration for the body resin alone may not cover a cap, seal or colored component sourced from another supplier. Use an annotated product drawing or approved sample to identify each part, then ask the factory to link the material specification and supporting evidence to that exact component."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Ask for a declaration tied to the exact product",
        "paragraph": "The most useful supplier statement identifies the product or model, material grade or component, intended food-contact conditions, supplier and document revision. Ask what regulations or authorizations support the claim, what limitations apply, and whether the statement covers the finished bottle or only a raw material. In the United States, FDA food-contact compliance is not a universal badge issued to every finished bottle; the regulatory status of substances and their authorized uses matters. Request a signed, product-specific letter or declaration and retain it with the quotation, sample approval and purchase order."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Read test reports for scope, not just the logo",
        "paragraph": "Check the report issuer, date, sample description, model or revision, test method, conditions, results and conclusion. The tested sample should match the body, cap, straw, gasket, color and intended use of the order closely enough to support the claim being made. Ask whether migration testing, repeated-use conditions, temperature, contact time or other parameters are relevant to the destination market. A report for a different bottle shape or a clear resin does not automatically prove a colored version is covered. If the scope is unclear, ask the laboratory or supplier to explain it before accepting the report."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Separate United States and European Union requests",
        "paragraph": "For a US-bound order, ask the supplier to identify the applicable FDA food-contact basis for each relevant substance and intended condition of use, with supporting declarations or documentation. For plastic food-contact articles placed on the EU market, Regulation (EU) No 10/2011 is a key specific measure and includes requirements for supporting documentation and a declaration of compliance where applicable. The exact document set depends on the product and regulatory scope, so do not treat a generic FDA statement as an EU declaration or the reverse. Confirm current rules with your compliance specialist or qualified adviser."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Connect documents to batches and change control",
        "paragraph": "Evidence is only useful when it can be connected to the product actually shipped. Record the model, resin supplier and grade where available, component revision, color or masterbatch, report number and approved sample ID. Ask how the supplier notifies buyers if a resin, cap, gasket, pigment, manufacturing site or design changes. A change can affect the relevance of earlier evidence even when the bottle looks similar. For repeat orders, keep a controlled document pack and require written review before an unapproved material substitution. This also makes it easier to investigate a complaint without guessing which version was delivered."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Put the evidence requirements into the purchase order",
        "paragraph": "Write the required documents and acceptance steps into the RFQ or purchase order rather than leaving them in a chat message. Identify the market, exact model and components, intended use, required declaration or test report, sample revision and delivery of final documents. State who reviews discrepancies and whether production may proceed while a document is missing. Avoid vague promises such as FDA approved, food grade or fully certified unless the supplier explains the exact legal basis and scope. A clear requirement lets the buyer compare factories on evidence quality, not on the confidence of a sales claim."
      }
    ],
    "checklist": [
      "Name the destination market and intended drink-use conditions",
      "List body, cap, straw, seal, valve and other contact components",
      "Identify material grades, suppliers and colorants where relevant",
      "Request a product-specific declaration with revision and scope",
      "Review test report sample identity, method, conditions and results",
      "Confirm the applicable US or EU regulatory route separately",
      "Match documents to the exact model and approved sample",
      "Record lot and component traceability for repeat orders",
      "Require written notice before material or component substitutions",
      "Keep the compliance pack with the RFQ, PO and sample approval"
    ],
    "faqs": [
      {
        "question": "Does an FDA logo or statement prove the finished bottle is approved?",
        "answer": "No. US food-contact compliance depends on the applicable authorization and intended conditions of use for relevant substances. Ask what the statement actually covers and request product-specific supporting documentation."
      },
      {
        "question": "Can one test report cover every color and lid option?",
        "answer": "Not automatically. The report scope, tested sample, components, colorants and conditions determine how far the evidence can be applied. Ask the supplier or laboratory to confirm coverage for the exact configuration."
      },
      {
        "question": "What should an EU buyer ask for?",
        "answer": "Ask whether Regulation (EU) No 10/2011 and other applicable food-contact rules cover the finished article, and request the relevant declaration of compliance and supporting documentation where required. Confirm the current requirements for your exact use."
      },
      {
        "question": "Should I accept a generic food-grade certificate?",
        "answer": "Only after checking its issuer, scope, date, product identity and legal relevance. A generic certificate may not identify the bottle, components, test conditions or destination-market requirements you need."
      }
    ],
