  {
    "slug": "custom-water-bottle-color-matching-pantone-guide",
    "title": "Custom Water Bottle Color Matching: Pantone, Samples and Production Control",
    "description": "Specify custom bottle colors more consistently with plastic color references, separate body and cap targets, physical samples, lighting and written tolerances.",
    "primary_keyword": "custom water bottle color matching",
    "secondary_keywords": [
      "Pantone plastic color matching",
      "custom bottle cap color",
      "plastic bottle color sample",
      "water bottle masterbatch color"
    ],
    "cover": "/assets/blog/custom-water-bottle-color-matching-20261018.webp",
    "coverAlt": "Six translucent reusable plastic sports bottles in different colors arranged with physical color chips and design samples; illustrative color-matching guide image",
    "category": "OEM & Branding",
    "intro": "A color that looks correct on a screen can look different when molded into a translucent plastic bottle, printed on a curved surface or paired with a separate cap. Buyers can reduce avoidable revisions by defining the reference, components, finish, lighting and approval sample before mass production. A color code is useful, but it does not remove the need to agree how that color should appear on the actual material. This guide explains how to prepare a practical color brief for a custom bottle project without promising an exact match before a sample has been reviewed.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Choose a reference designed for the right material",
        "paragraph": "Begin with a specific physical color standard or a clearly identified target sample. A Pantone code from a paper guide can help communicate design intent, but paper ink and molded plastic are different substrates and processes. Pantone also offers plastic chips intended for communicating and evaluating color on plastic products. Tell the supplier which system and edition you are referencing, and provide the code plus a physical chip or approved sample when color is commercially important. Do not rely on a screenshot, phone image or uncalibrated monitor as the only acceptance standard."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Specify the bottle body, cap and accessories separately",
        "paragraph": "List the target color for each visible component: body, cap, straw, carry loop, gasket if colored, and printed artwork. These parts may use different resins, colorants or manufacturing processes, so one code does not guarantee that all components will look identical. State whether the body should be transparent, translucent or opaque, and whether the cap should match exactly or intentionally contrast. If the logo is printed rather than molded into the plastic, define its ink reference and location separately. An annotated drawing or component table helps the factory quote the right work."
      },
      {
        "heading": "Approve a physical color sample before bulk production",
